# Scripts with no direct executable route in the covered build graph or native
# sequential driver, and the reason each remains a manual interface. The gate
# checks this direct-route boundary, not arbitrary shell control flow. Fixture
# copies and nested helper calls can have behavioral tests without authorizing
# the corresponding live operation as an automated route.
#
# One row per (script, reason slug), with the finding above it. A row is a claim
# that this script is a tool a developer runs by hand, not a gate that was
# written and wired nowhere. The distinction matters: a gate nothing invokes
# reports nothing and is then cited as coverage, which is how
# scripts/test-zruntime.zig's script sat in the tree pointing at a root file
# that had been deleted.
#
# The list only ratchets down. Adding a row is a deliberate widening.

# Manual macOS experiment in dyld load overhead. Builds and times the binary;
# asserts nothing, so there is no verdict for a gate to consume.

# Two lines: build a TSX example ReleaseFast and serve it on port 3000. A
# convenience for looking at the JSX frontend by hand; it blocks on a server, so
# nothing automated can wait on it.

# Developer machine setup. Runs before the tree can be built at all, so no build
# step or gate could depend on it.
setup.sh developer-setup

# Regenerates docs/restrictions-to-proofs.md from restriction_registry.zig. The
# drift between the two is already gated - check-docs-drift.sh compares the
# rendered table against the registry row by row - so this script is the
# author-side fix for a failure that gate reports, not a check of its own.
update-restrictions-doc.sh regeneration-tool

# The proof gate a user's pull request workflow runs. Users copy it with the
# example workflow docs/examples/proof-gate.yml; this repository runs no
# workflow (D-009, D13). The verdict logic it calls, `zttp proofs gate`, is
# tested in packages/runtime/src/proofs/pr_gate.zig.
proof-gate.sh user-template

# Stable operator entry points; the native driver owns their executable routes.
verify.sh canonical-verification-entrypoint
release.sh release-operation
# Author-side publication and golden regeneration require deliberate invocation.
# Fixture tests also exercise publishers, but a copied-script fixture is not a
# route authorizing live publication in the current working tree.
update-convergence.sh publication-operation
update-coverage.sh publication-operation
update-contract-goldens.sh golden-regeneration
update-expert-goldens.sh golden-regeneration
# Standalone helpers also exercised by wrappers or release fixture copies. The
# graph census proves direct execution only, not arbitrary shell control flow.
check-module-boundary.sh standalone-boundary-helper-with-wrapper-tests
render-release-notes.sh standalone-release-renderer-with-fixture-tests
zts-import-graph.sh import-graph-inspection
